X('house-bill-of-lading-vs-master-bill-of-lading',[
 ('How the two fit together',['When a forwarder consolidates cargo from several shippers into one container, the shipping line issues a master bill of lading to the forwarder, as shipper. The forwarder then issues a house bill of lading to each of its customers. The buyer in the destination country receives the cargo against the house bill, while the forwarder\'s agent holds or releases the master.','The cargo is the same, but there are two layers of contract, and each has its own shipper and consignee.']),
 ('A simple example',['Three Indian exporters ship small lots of garments to a buyer in Hamburg through one forwarder. The line issues one master bill, naming the forwarder as shipper and its Hamburg agent as consignee. The forwarder issues three house bills, one for each exporter, naming each Hamburg buyer as consignee. Each exporter sends its own house bill to its own buyer, and the agent in Hamburg releases each lot once the right bill is surrendered.']),
 ('What to check',['The names, ports and cargo description on the house bill match the invoice and the letter of credit, if one is used.','The house bill is issued by a forwarder that you can identify and that holds the right licence or registration.','If your bank needs a bill from the actual carrier, check whether a house bill is acceptable under the credit terms.'])],
 [('Which bill do I need to clear the cargo?','The consignee on the house bill needs the house bill, or the release instruction the forwarder agrees, to collect the cargo. The master bill is held between the line and the forwarder.')])
X('telex-release-and-surrendered-bill-of-lading',[
 ('The flow in steps',['The shipper asks the line or forwarder to release the cargo without originals. The shipper surrenders the full set of original bills at the origin, and the carrier sends a message to the destination agent to release the cargo to the named consignee. The consignee shows identity and pays any charges, and the goods are released against the delivery order.','Because the original bill is no longer in circulation, the cargo can be released before documents arrive by courier, which helps when the voyage is short.']),
 ('Who takes the risk',['The shipper gives up the control that an original bill provides. If the buyer has not paid, the shipper has no paper to hold. Use it when payment has been received, or the buyer is well known and the terms are open account. For a new buyer or a letter of credit that requires an original, use the normal bill.']),
 ('Practical tips',['Name the right consignee on the bill from the start. A telex release cannot easily be redirected to another party.','Pay the origin charges early. Carriers often will not release the telex until they are paid.','Keep the surrender receipt, because it is your evidence that the originals were returned.'])],
 [('Is a telex release the same as a sea waybill?','They give a similar result, a release without original documents, but they are different. A telex release is a way of releasing cargo under a bill of lading that was surrendered. A sea waybill is a document that was never negotiable.')])
X('sea-waybill-vs-bill-of-lading',[
 ('Choosing in practice',['A bill of lading is a document of title. Whoever holds the original can claim the cargo, and it can be endorsed to a bank or a new buyer, which is why banks like it for letters of credit and for goods sold while at sea. A sea waybill is a receipt and a contract of carriage that names a consignee and gives no control to the holder, so the goods go to the named party.','For short routes and trusted buyers, a waybill avoids the delay of sending originals. For a first sale, a high-value shipment or a letter of credit, a bill of lading offers stronger protection.']),
 ('Things to confirm',['What the sales contract or the letter of credit demands. Many credits specify the bill of lading and will not accept a waybill.','Whether the buyer needs a document of title to sell the cargo on or to finance it.','The carrier\'s terms for issuing each document, since a few lines charge a fee for one or the other.']),
 ('Risk summary',['A waybill is faster and cheaper but leaves the seller with little control once the cargo is shipped. A bill of lading gives control but can be lost in the post and takes time to arrive. Choose according to how much you trust the buyer and how you will be paid.'])],
 [('Can I use a sea waybill with a letter of credit?','Only if the credit allows it. Many credits ask for a full set of original bills of lading, so check the wording before you choose.')])
X('commercial-invoice-requirements',[
 ('What each party looks at',['Customs looks at the description, the value, the terms of sale and the country of origin, because they decide the duty. The bank looks at whether the invoice agrees with the letter of credit or the contract. The buyer looks at the price and the quantity for payment. A good invoice answers all three.']),
 ('A checklist before you issue it',['Full names and addresses of seller and buyer, and the consignee if different.','Invoice number and date, and the purchase order reference.','A clear description of each item, with the model or grade, and the HS code if you know it.','Quantity, unit price, total, currency and the Incoterm with the named place.','Country of origin, and the payment terms.','Signature or stamp where the buyer or the credit requires it.']),
 ('Value that causes questions',['A very low price without an explanation can bring a valuation query. If the goods are a sample, a replacement or free of charge, say so on the invoice and give a fair value for customs. Never show two different invoices for the same shipment, because that is treated as a serious offence.'])],
 [('Does the invoice need to show the HS code?','It is not always required by law, but it is very helpful, and some countries and many letters of credit ask for it. It reduces mistakes and delays at clearance.')])
X('packing-list-guide',[
 ('What a good list contains',['Each package marked and numbered, with the contents of each.','Gross and net weight and the dimensions of every package, in the same units as the booking.','The marks and numbers on the packages, which must agree with the bill of lading.','The invoice number and date, so the two documents match.']),
 ('Where it is used',['By the forwarder, to book the right amount of space and to plan the loading.','By Customs, to check the physical goods against the declaration.','By the buyer, to receive and count the goods at the warehouse.','By the insurer, if a claim has to prove what was shipped.']),
 ('Common errors',['Totals that do not add up, or weights that differ from the invoice and the bill of lading.','Missing package numbers, which makes a partial loss hard to prove.','A packing list that is different from what the supplier actually packed. Check it against the goods before they leave, because a customs examination will compare the two.'])],
 [('Is a packing list the same as a commercial invoice?','No. The invoice shows the price and the terms of the sale. The packing list shows how the goods are packed, with weights and measures. Both are needed for most shipments.')])
X('proforma-invoice-vs-commercial-invoice',[
 ('Why the difference matters',['A proforma invoice is an offer: a statement of what the seller will supply and at what price. It is used to agree the deal, to open a letter of credit or to apply for an import licence or a foreign exchange payment. A commercial invoice is issued when the goods ship and is the legal record of the sale. Customs uses the commercial invoice to assess the duty.','Using the proforma as the invoice for clearance can cause problems, because it is not a final record of the sale.']),
 ('Moving from one to the other',['The commercial invoice should follow the proforma on price and description unless something changed, and any change should be explained. A large gap between the two without a reason can draw questions from the bank and from Customs.']),
 ('Tips',['Mark the proforma clearly as a proforma and not as a tax invoice.','State the validity period of the offer, since prices can move.','Keep both documents together in the shipment file.'])],
 [('Can I clear goods with a proforma invoice?','Normally no. Customs expects a commercial invoice for the actual sale. A proforma can be used only in special cases, such as samples, with the right declarations.')])
X('letter-of-credit-basics',[
 ('A short walk-through',['The buyer asks its bank to issue the letter of credit in favour of the seller. The seller\'s bank advises it, and the seller ships the goods and presents the documents the credit asks for. If the documents comply exactly with the credit, the bank pays the seller or accepts the draft for later payment.','Banks check the documents and not the goods. A shipment that is perfect but has a document that does not match the credit can still be refused payment.']),
 ('Why documents get refused',['A late shipment or presentation outside the dates in the credit.','Different spellings of names or addresses on the invoice and the bill of lading.','A missing or wrongly worded certificate, or a different quantity or description from the credit.','A bill of lading that is claused or shows a different port from the credit.']),
 ('Good habits',['Read the credit as soon as it arrives and ask for an amendment at once if any term cannot be met.','Ask your forwarder to draft the transport document to the terms of the credit, not the other way round.','Check every document against the credit before you present it, and allow time to correct errors.'])],
 [('Does a letter of credit guarantee payment?','It is a bank undertaking to pay against complying documents, subject to the bank\'s own standing. It does not guarantee payment if the documents do not comply.')])
X('shipping-instructions-and-cut-off',[
 ('What goes into the SI',['Shipper, consignee and notify party, with full addresses.','Description of goods, HS code, marks and numbers, gross weight and the number of packages.','Container details if known, freight terms (prepaid or collect) and the number of original bills required.','Any special wording needed for a letter of credit or the buyer\'s clearance.']),
 ('Why the SI cut-off matters',['The line uses the SI to prepare the manifest and the draft bill of lading. If the SI comes after the cut-off, the cargo can be rolled to a later vessel, or you may pay for a late amendment. Customs also needs the data for the export manifest, so late or wrong data can delay clearance at both ends.']),
 ('Reducing amendments',['Check the draft bill of lading as soon as it arrives. Every change after that point can cost a fee, and some lines charge more after the vessel has sailed. A name or number that is wrong on the bill of lading can also hold up the buyer\'s payment, so the few minutes needed for a careful check are well spent.'])],
 [('What happens if I miss the SI cut-off?','The line may refuse the instructions for that vessel, charge a late fee, or roll the cargo to the next sailing. Ask your forwarder for the cut-off with the booking.')])
X('delivery-order-explained',[
 ('How it fits into the import process',['After the vessel arrives and the import manifest is filed, the importer or its broker takes the original bill of lading (or a telex release) to the shipping line or its agent. The line checks the documents, collects its charges and issues the delivery order. The order is then presented at the terminal or CFS, together with the customs out of charge, to take delivery of the cargo.','Without it, the terminal cannot release the container.']),
 ('Charges at this stage',['Ocean freight if collect, and terminal handling charges at the destination.','Documentation, delivery order and any other local charges set by the line.','A container deposit or detention charges if the container is held beyond the free days.']),
 ('Keeping it quick',['Ask your broker to confirm the line\'s requirements before the vessel arrives.','Pay the charges and collect the delivery order on the day of arrival if the customs clearance is ready.','Check the free days on the container. Every day after them adds cost.'])],
 [('Who issues the delivery order?','The shipping line or its agent in the port of discharge, against the original bill of lading or release and the payment of its charges.')])
X('import-export-manifest-igm-egm',[
 ('What goes into a manifest',['The carrier lists the cargo on the vessel or flight: the bill of lading numbers, shippers, consignees, a description and the weights, with the container numbers. For imports, this is the import general manifest, filed before arrival. For exports, the export general manifest is filed after the cargo is loaded.','Customs uses the manifest to link the bills of entry and shipping bills to the cargo actually on board.']),
 ('Typical problems',['A bill of lading number or consignee name that does not match the bill of entry. The system cannot link them and the clearance stops.','Cargo missing from the manifest, or entered under the wrong container.','For exports, a shipping bill that has not been matched with the manifest, which can block drawback and RODTEP.']),
 ('What to do',['Check the details on the draft bill of lading, because the manifest is built from it.','Ask your forwarder or the line to correct errors as soon as they are found, since amendments need approvals and take time.','For exports, confirm the manifest has been filed before you chase the refund or incentive.'])],
 [('What is the difference between IGM and EGM?','The IGM is the import general manifest, filed for the cargo arriving. The EGM is the export general manifest, filed for the cargo leaving India.')])
X('clean-vs-claused-bill-of-lading',[
 ('What triggers a clause',['The carrier or its agent notes a visible problem when receiving the cargo: damaged packaging, wet or stained cartons, dented drums, missing seals or a shortage in the count. Notes such as "carton torn" or "rusty drums" turn a clean bill into a claused one. The clause does not say who caused the damage, only that the cargo looked that way at loading.','Banks usually will not accept a claused bill under a letter of credit that asks for a clean one.']),
 ('How to avoid it',['Inspect the cargo at the factory and before it is stuffed. Fix damaged packages before loading.','Use packing that suits the route and the mode, and photograph the loading.','Make sure the cargo count agrees with the documents before the container is sealed.']),
 ('If it happens',['Ask for the cargo to be repacked or replaced, and request a clean bill if the problem is fixed. Some shippers give a letter of indemnity to the carrier to obtain a clean bill, but this carries legal risk and should be taken only with advice, since it can be treated as hiding damage.'])],
 [('Can I get a clean bill after a clause is added?','Sometimes, if the defect is corrected and the carrier agrees. A letter of indemnity is a risky shortcut and should not be used without legal advice.')])
